WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.700
My family left Korea

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.533
when I was seven years old,

00:00:03.566 --> 00:00:05.666
and I never really took the time

00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:07.733
to learn its history

00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:10.433
and culture until a few years ago.

00:00:19.666 --> 00:00:20.966
You probably know of Korea

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.033
as two different countries

00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:25.633
as North and South Korea,

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:29.566
but relative to its overall history,

00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:32.300
which begins as early as 700 BC,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.833
this is a fairly recent occurrence

00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:39.900
and actually for much of its history

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.533
it's been attacked and controlled

00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:42.900
by foreign forces

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.866
and has had to constantly fight for

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.800
independence. And today

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:48.600
I'm going to be sharing

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.300
a project that I did in my 4th

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.933
year of architecture school

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.400
that's taught me a bit

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.600
about the painful history of Korea,

00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:58.166
An has made me think a little deeper

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.533
about the meaning of a shared identity,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.066
as well as the role of architecture

00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:05.066
in addressing complex

00:01:05.066 --> 00:01:07.500
cultural and political conflicts.

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.366
In 1910, Korea was invaded

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.600
and colonized by Japan

00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:19.300
until 1945 when Japan

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.600
was defeated by the Allied forces

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.000
in the Second World War,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.900
Korea was liberated

00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:27.500
from Japanese rule, but the US

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:30.466
and the Soviet Union

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.600
both wanted a piece

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.766
of Korea, so they decided

00:01:33.766 --> 00:01:36.000
that the country will be split in half.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.300
the Soviets occupying the North

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:40.600
and the Americans occupying the South.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.466
They simply drew a line across the map

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.700
and decided to divide the country

00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:47.100
along the 38th Parallel,

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.466
which has now become the DMZ.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.066
Of course, the Koreans didn't

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.566
get a say in any of this.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.066
the Soviets helped elect a communist

00:01:56.066 --> 00:01:57.900
guerrilla leader into office

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.900
and the Americans helped a US

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.300
educated Democratic leader into office.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.466
Since then, both the North and South

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.033
desperately wanted a united Korea,

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:11.500
but of course under their own

00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:13.400
rule and political regime,

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.600
and there was a lot of tension

00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:16.966
between the two sides.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.600
In 1953, the North Korean leader

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:21.000
tried to overthrow the South

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.533
Korean government in an attempt

00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.166
to unify the two countries,

00:02:24.166 --> 00:02:26.433
which led to the Korean War

00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:29.133
pitting Koreans against Koreans.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.566
Since then, the two countries have been

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.066
in a perpetual state of war,

00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:36.566
forcing young Korean men in their 20s

00:02:36.566 --> 00:02:38.233
into mandatory military

00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.333
service for 18 months,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.600
leaving a pretty big gap

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.366
in its economy and social structure.

00:02:45.466 --> 00:02:47.766
If you are a fan of Korean dramas,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.866
this also sometimes serves

00:02:49.866 --> 00:02:52.733
as a convenient resolution to the plot.

00:02:52.766 --> 00:02:54.433
So while the two countries

00:02:54.433 --> 00:02:56.066
still use the same language,

00:02:56.166 --> 00:02:57.833
they've diverged on

00:02:57.866 --> 00:02:59.333
pretty much all aspects,

00:02:59.366 --> 00:03:01.733
including political ideology,

00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:03.700
culture and economy.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:06.733
For most South Koreans, reunification

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.400
is an unrealistic, impractical,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.100
and even a contentious idea.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:15.066
The vast majority of the South

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.800
Korean population today,

00:03:16.833 --> 00:03:18.900
especially the younger generation,

00:03:18.933 --> 00:03:20.600
oppose reunification

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.466
just until the late 90s.

00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:23.700
There was around 80%

00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:25.766
support from South Koreans

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:26.966
for reunification,

00:03:26.966 --> 00:03:29.833
but a poll in 2011 showed that

00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:33.500
the number had been reduced to 56%.

00:04:15.733 --> 00:04:18.133
People in my grandparents generation,

00:04:18.166 --> 00:04:20.866
however, still have long lost

00:04:20.866 --> 00:04:22.033
family members on the

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:23.233
other side of the border.

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:25.133
This is the footage

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:27.300
from a rare reunion in 2018

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.200
that was granted by the North

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.133
Korean government.

00:04:37.766 --> 00:04:38.766
The families were

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.033
reunited after 70 years

00:04:41.066 --> 00:04:43.200
and were given 11 hours together

00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:44.900
over the time span of three days

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:45.900
to see each other,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.433
with no repeat visits allowed.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.500
The families were selected

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.733
based on a lottery and there are 56,000

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.633
Koreans on the waiting list

00:04:56.666 --> 00:04:58.966
to be picked for visits like this.

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:00.433
Every year, around

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.766
3000 of these people die,

00:05:02.766 --> 00:05:04.500
reducing the number of families

00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:06.200
waiting to be reunited.

00:05:16.066 --> 00:05:18.933
This event was the result of a government

00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:21.633
led process towards reunification,

00:05:21.633 --> 00:05:22.866
which was started in 2000,

00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:26.733
in the June 15th 

00:05:26.733 --> 00:05:29.000
North - South joint Declaration

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:30.566
where both governments agree

00:05:30.566 --> 00:05:32.333
to promote reunification

00:05:32.333 --> 00:05:34.000
in their respective countries.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.500
I also remember pretty clearly

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.200
as a kid in elementary school

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.600
learning about our friendly neighbors

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:41.433
in the North

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.800
and learning songs about reunification.

00:06:01.366 --> 00:06:03.366
Practical and economic concerns

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.466
aside, in instances

00:06:05.466 --> 00:06:07.200
when the two Koreans meet,

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.466
it's been shown over and over again

00:06:09.466 --> 00:06:11.600
that it's an unexplainable

00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:13.366
emotional experience.

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:08.100
Well, the reunification still remains

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:09.900
a very lofty goal,

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.000
something on the very far horizon

00:07:13.033 --> 00:07:14.300
for both nations.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.300
One of the efforts to strengthen

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.800
the North - South relations

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.600
was the push to create

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.300
industrial and economic ties

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.500
by establishing areas like

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.533
the Kaesong Industrial Park

00:07:26.566 --> 00:07:27.600
which would allow

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.766
South Korean companies to employ

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.000
cheap labor in North Korea

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.366
while creating jobs and income

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.900
for the North Korean economy.

00:07:36.766 --> 00:07:38.600
Two years after its opening,

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:39.800
the number of North Korean

00:07:39.833 --> 00:07:42.400
staff hit 10,000 workers

00:07:42.466 --> 00:07:45.133
a year after it doubled, and in 2012,

00:07:45.133 --> 00:07:47.500
it reached over 50,000.

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:49.766
The accumulated earnings

00:07:49.766 --> 00:07:52.000
from goods produced at the complex rose

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.900
from some 100,000,000 US dollars in 2007

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:59.333
to 2 billion dollars in 2013.

00:07:59.333 --> 00:08:00.800
The park is located

00:08:00.833 --> 00:08:03.666
10 kilometers North of the DMZ

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:05.300
an it's the only direct

00:08:05.333 --> 00:08:06.900
road and rail access

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.600
between North and South Korea.

00:08:08.933 --> 00:08:12.800
The DMZ, the Korean Demilitarized Zone,

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.466
is an incredibly unique

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.033
condition of its own.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.300
It's been a four kilometer

00:08:18.333 --> 00:08:20.500
buffer between North and South Korea

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.500
since the Korean War in 1953.

00:08:23.766 --> 00:08:25.433
And this restricted land

00:08:25.433 --> 00:08:27.233
hasn't been altered by human hands

00:08:27.266 --> 00:08:29.500
for the past 70 years,

00:08:29.533 --> 00:08:32.066
and as a result, has created

00:08:32.066 --> 00:08:33.766
a natural paradise

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:36.933
for an incredibly diverse range of animal

00:08:36.933 --> 00:08:38.800
and plant species that have

00:08:38.966 --> 00:08:40.200
pretty much vanished

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.133
in many other parts of the world.

00:09:04.833 --> 00:09:06.800
However, the barbed wire

00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:08.000
fence that runs along

00:09:08.033 --> 00:09:10.200
the DMZ is impenetrable

00:09:10.266 --> 00:09:13.533
not only for people but also for animals,

00:09:13.633 --> 00:09:15.700
and this has created a fragmented

00:09:15.733 --> 00:09:17.400
landscape between the two

00:09:17.433 --> 00:09:19.066
mega ecosystems,

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:20.866
limiting the possibility

00:09:20.866 --> 00:09:22.900
of further growth. 

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.066
When connected,

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.866
The ecosystem has huge

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.666
environmental possibilities

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:28.866
and as a whole, 

00:09:28.866 --> 00:09:30.566
could become the starting point

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.500
for a natural wildlife corridor

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.566
extending to the rest of Asia.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.666
In 2014, during my 4th year studio,

00:09:37.666 --> 00:09:39.833
we were given a project brief

00:09:39.866 --> 00:09:42.133
which was to create a border crossing

00:09:42.166 --> 00:09:44.333
between any two countries.

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:46.133
I decided on the border

00:09:46.166 --> 00:09:48.133
between North and South Korea,

00:09:48.133 --> 00:09:50.566
not just for personal reasons,

00:09:50.566 --> 00:09:53.366
but for the complex social,

00:09:53.366 --> 00:09:55.700
economic and environmental conditions.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.333
Based on my research,

00:09:57.333 --> 00:09:59.866
I wanted to create a commercial border

00:09:59.866 --> 00:10:02.200
crossing on the existing road

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.200
to the industrial region,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.266
but at the same time wanted

00:10:06.266 --> 00:10:08.633
to address the separation of

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.300
the wildlife corridor

00:10:10.333 --> 00:10:12.000
by the barbed wire fence.

00:10:12.033 --> 00:10:13.766
The separation of the wildlife

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.333
corridor by the road

00:10:15.333 --> 00:10:17.600
connecting the industrial regions

00:10:17.633 --> 00:10:20.466
by also introducing an Animal Crossing.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.766
This Animal Crossing

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.366
would be structurally supported

00:10:23.366 --> 00:10:25.766
by public observation towers

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.733
on either sides of the border.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.066
As the two buildings hold up and connect

00:10:31.066 --> 00:10:32.900
the broken wildlife corridors

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:34.233
in four directions,

00:10:34.666 --> 00:10:36.866
it would, first of all, provide

00:10:36.866 --> 00:10:38.966
a public attraction that would

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.433
allow us to admire the wildlife,

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:44.900
let us witness the economic activity

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:48.166
that's happening and at the same time,

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.200
provide a place where we can gaze over

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:51.933
to the other side of the border,

00:10:52.166 --> 00:10:54.066
where, in the least,

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.233
it could be the beginning of some kind of

00:10:57.266 --> 00:10:58.533
kindred emotions.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.000
This actually isn't my typical process

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.266
for designing a building,

00:11:05.266 --> 00:11:06.733
but on this project

00:11:06.766 --> 00:11:08.566
there was a descriptive word

00:11:08.566 --> 00:11:10.200
that kept coming to my mind

00:11:10.233 --> 00:11:12.400
as I mulled over the conditions.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:13.900
In Korean,

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.500
you would call this gin-jang, 

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.333
loosely translating into

00:11:18.333 --> 00:11:21.100
tension.

00:11:21.133 --> 00:11:22.700
Tension, Miriam Webster describes

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.300
as: a state of latent hostility

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.100
or opposition between

00:11:28.133 --> 00:11:30.033
individuals or groups.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.000
It's also described as:

00:11:32.033 --> 00:11:33.866
Inner striving, unrest

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.600
or imbalance, often with physiological

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.700
indication of emotion.

00:11:38.933 --> 00:11:40.266
It's also the act

00:11:40.266 --> 00:11:43.666
or action of stretching, or the condition

00:11:43.666 --> 00:11:44.866
or degree of being

00:11:44.866 --> 00:11:47.000
stretched to stiffness.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:48.233
And finally it's

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.700
either of two balancing forces

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:53.400
causing or tending to cause extension.

00:12:02.033 --> 00:12:02.966
Of course,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.700
the bridge would be in most parts

00:12:05.733 --> 00:12:06.833
self supporting,

00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:08.966
with a substantial structure,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.866
but I felt like the symbolic gesture

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:12.900
of the two buildings

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.266
holding up the bridge, even in some minor way,

00:12:16.266 --> 00:12:18.066
added a powerful narrative

00:12:18.066 --> 00:12:19.900
that I didn't really want to compromise.

00:12:20.166 --> 00:12:23.333
And the motion of pulling and tension

00:12:23.366 --> 00:12:26.300
in large part, drove the design

00:12:26.333 --> 00:12:28.033
of the individual buildings

00:12:28.066 --> 00:12:30.966
as I went through multiple iterations.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.233
The two buildings are identical

00:12:33.266 --> 00:12:35.666
and they torque as it goes up,

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.033
which means that each floor plate

00:12:38.066 --> 00:12:40.466
rotates at an angle, providing

00:12:40.466 --> 00:12:42.933
unique views on each floor,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:45.500
like views to the mountains,

00:12:45.633 --> 00:12:49.066
views of the city on the other side of the border,

00:12:49.066 --> 00:12:53.000
views to the building across the border, 

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.500
views to the Animal Crossing,

00:12:55.933 --> 00:12:57.100
and we also have views

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.533
for the border control.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.633
Maybe the possibility of a united

00:13:04.633 --> 00:13:06.333
Korea is long gone.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.533
Maybe the two countries have drifted

00:13:09.566 --> 00:13:11.466
way too far apart,

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:13.400
that reunification is

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:16.133
an unrealistic proposition.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.566
But our history is always a part of us,

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.966
and if nothing else,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.566
maybe it could at least provide a place

00:13:27.566 --> 00:13:29.900
where people can experience

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:31.600
the beautiful surroundings

00:13:32.066 --> 00:13:34.666
and reflect on our collective memory

00:13:34.666 --> 00:13:36.733
and ponder on what it means to

00:13:36.733 --> 00:13:38.166
have a shared identity.

